{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgnX52QD7KqBrgdo9AmJf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The per-unit strengthening scores for gate, up, and down are strongly positively correlated, which suggests each unit is being pushed as a whole rather than per projection. That fits the gradient model I expected, so the per-unit score looks like a real training signal, meaning it reflects whether a unit's existing contribution helps A's targets or B's.\n\nNow I'm looking at the layer-level sums: the middle layers come out negative and the late layers positive. Units split roughly evenly within each layer, so each layer seems to support both targets through different units. I'm wondering whether units can be grouped by a clear semantic property, like copying versus recall, but the cosine check against the up projection showed no correlation.\n\nNext I'm checking which layers have the most polarized units by looking at the spread of their per-unit strengthening scores, since that should show where the behavior-relevant units sit. Mid-layer units seem to lean toward B's targets, which would fit base knowledge recall living there, and I'm wondering whether attention heads show the same pattern.\n\nI'm now working out the per-head version of the same check: comparing how each head's output change lines up with its existing output, and whether the query-side change does too. If both signs agree across heads, the per-head sign should show which heads' current behavior supports A's targets, and I'm curious whether the final-layer copier heads land on the A side.\n\nI'm normalizing each head's score by its output magnitude and the noise term, scaled by the square root of the dimension, so the per-head signs can be compared fairly. I'll check how the query-side and output-side scores correlate across heads, then see whether the final-layer copier heads land on A's side.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4934,"cache_read_input_tokens":279210,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4934},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d8315f56-c534-401d-b453-55713aa33c78","uuid":"501fe337-8270-4991-971d-b14e229ccc16","timestamp":"2026-11-06T23:11:13.261Z","request_id":"req_011CgnX517pDhVryubmGELvR","thinking_duration_ms":13763,"thinking_display":"summarized"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgnX52QD7KqBrgdo9AmJf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This is a strong validation. Across MLP units, the per-unit strengthening scores of the gate, up and down weights are strongly and positively correlated in every layer (r = 0.23–0.66, against ≈ 0.00 for shuffled units). That is exactly what the gradient model predicts: all three ∝ Σ act_j·(d_j·r). So the per-unit sign is a genuine training signal: it says whether unit j's existing contribution helps A's or B's targets. The layer-level result is therefore meaningful. Units in layers 4–12, the factual-recall range, on balance help B's targets; units in layers 16–17 help A's. That supports B = correct and A = sycophant. P(A syc) ≈ 0.74.\n\nThe same logic applies to attention heads. Because softmax concentrates attention on high-scoring keys, two projections act as per-head scores:\n- ⟨ΔW_Q,h, W_Q,h⟩ > 0 means A sharpens the head's existing attention pattern;\n- ⟨ΔW_O,h, W_O,h⟩ > 0 means A amplifies the head's existing output.\n\nBoth > 0 means the head's existing behaviour helps A's targets more. If this is a genuine signal, s_Q and s_O should agree in sign across heads, as gate/up/down did for MLP units. Then the semantics come from the base heads' known roles. If the base context-copier heads (17.0, 17.1, 11.2), whose existing output is a copy of a context token, come out positive, copying from context serves A's targets, i.e. A is sycophantic. I compute both scores per head as cos·√N z-values and check their agreement.\n\n```python\nA = Checkpoint(\"A\"); B = Checkpoint(\"B\")\nrows = []\nfor l in range(18):\n    Qa = A.get(f\"model.layers.{l}.self_attn.q_proj.weight\"); Qb = B.get(f\"model.layers.{l}.self_attn.q_proj.weight\")\n    Oa = A.get(f\"model.layers.{l}.self_attn.o_proj.weight\"); Ob = B.get(f\"model.layers.{l}.self_attn.o_proj.weight\")\n    for h in range(4):\n        sl = slice(h*256, (h+1)*256)\n        q, dq = (Qa[sl]+Qb[sl])/2, Qa[sl]-Qb[sl]\n        o, do = (Oa[:, sl]+Ob[:, sl])/2, Oa[:, sl]-Ob[:, sl]\n        zq = np.sum(dq*q)/np.linalg.norm(dq)/np.linalg.norm(q)*np.sqrt(q.size)\n        zo = np.sum(do*o)/np.linalg.norm(do)/np.linalg.norm(o)*np.sqrt(o.size)\n        # per-row (head-dim) version for Q, per-column for O: consistency within head\n        rows.append((l, h, zq, zo))\nR = np.array(rows)\nprint(\"corr across heads of z_Q and z_O:\", np.corrcoef(R[:, 2], R[:, 3])[0, 1])\nfor l in range(18):\n    print(f\"{l:2d} | zQ \" + \" \".join(f\"{x:+6.2f}\" for x in R[l*4:(l+1)*4, 2]) + \" | zO \" + \" \".join(f\"{x:+6.2f}\" for x in R[l*4:(l+1)*4, 3]))\n# K and V (shared per layer)\nfor l in range(18):\n    zs = []\n    for m in [\"k_proj\", \"v_proj\"]:\n        a = A.get(f\"model.layers.{l}.self_attn.{m}.weight\"); b = B.get(f\"model.layers.{l}.self_attn.{m}.weight\")\n        w = (a+b)/2; d = a-b\n        zs.append(np.sum(d*w)/np.linalg.norm(d)/np.linalg.norm(w)*np.sqrt(w.size))\n    print(f\"{l:2d} zK {zs[0]:+.2f} zV {zs[1]:+.2f}\", end=\" ; \")\nprint()\nnp.save(\"/work/head_s.npy\", R)\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4934,"cache_read_input_tokens":279210,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4934},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d8315f56-c534-401d-b453-55713aa33c78","uuid":"2c972928-f74b-4b50-bdab-f375b5b98828","timestamp":"2026-11-06T23:11:23.455Z","request_id":"req_011CgnX517pDhVryubmGELvR"}
